---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const wrap = 'mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-16 lg:py-20';
const kicker = 'font-sans text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-navy-600';
const h2c = 'mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-medium leading-tight tracking-tight text-navy sm:text-4xl';
const lede = 'mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft';
const h3c = 'mt-10 font-serif text-xl font-medium text-navy';
const pc = 'mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft';
const mcard = 'my-6 rounded-lg border border-line border-l-[3px] border-l-brass bg-paper-raised p-6 shadow-sm';
const tag = 'font-sans text-[0.7rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-brass-deep';
const olc = 'mt-3 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-[0.97rem] text-ink-soft';
const th = 'border-b border-line px-3.5 py-2.5 text-left font-sans text-[0.7rem] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint';
const td = 'border-b border-line px-3.5 py-2.5 align-top text-[0.95rem] text-ink-soft';
---

<Base
  title="GTM Field Manual | Levi Henry Group"
  description="A working reference for founders taking a platform to market: the GTM toolkit, first-customers playbook, startup and founder types, and an industry field guide."
  noindex={true}
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main">
    <!-- HERO -->
    <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 pt-14 pb-8 lg:pt-20">
      <p data-reveal class={kicker}>Field guide · For founders</p>
      <h1 data-reveal class="mt-4 max-w-[20ch] font-serif text-4xl font-medium leading-[1.07] tracking-tight text-navy sm:text-5xl">The GTM Field Manual</h1>
      <p data-reveal class="mt-5 text-lg text-ink-soft">A working reference for taking a platform to market: the frameworks, the first-customer playbooks, and how the game changes by startup type and industry. It's the same thinking behind the GTM consulting work at Levi Henry Group.</p>
      <div data-reveal class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-x-6 gap-y-2 border-t border-line pt-5 font-sans text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-faint">
        <span>Go-to-market</span><span>First customers</span><span>Cross-industry</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 1 -->
    <section class="border-t border-line">
      <div class={wrap}>
        <p class={kicker}>01 · The toolkit</p>
        <h2 class={h2c}>Five decisions before you can sell on purpose</h2>
        <p class={lede}>Before a founder can sell deliberately instead of by accident, five things have to be settled: who to sell to, how to talk about it, what to charge, where to find buyers, and which motion fits the product.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Ideal customer profile (ICP)</h3>
        <p class={pc}>Precision beats breadth. An ICP isn't a broad segment ("mid-market SaaS"). It's a narrow description of whoever gets the most value, fastest, at the lowest cost to win and serve. Build it <em>while</em> you build the product, so it drives what you make, not just who you email.</p>
        <div class={mcard}>
          <div class={tag}>Framework · RaceCapital's 9 parameters</div>
          <h4 class="mt-2 font-sans font-bold text-navy">Nine questions that define an ICP</h4>
          <ol class={olc}>
            <li>Industry: target verticals, and explicit exclusions</li>
            <li>Geography: where you focus</li>
            <li>Company size: individual, startup, SMB, or enterprise</li>
            <li>Budget: what the market already expects to pay</li>
            <li>Decision makers: the buyer versus the actual user</li>
            <li>Buying process: direct purchase or committee procurement</li>
            <li>Pain points: the specific problem you solve</li>
            <li>Business goals: the outcome they're chasing</li>
            <li>Technology: required integrations and existing stack</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <p class={pc}>A real ICP comes from three signals (First Round Review): interviewing the people who feel the pain most, analysing which deals actually close fastest and biggest, and (most useful early) situational triggers that predict urgency ("companies with 20+ open remote roles" beats "large company"). "Spray and pray" with no ICP is the single most common early failure.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Positioning</h3>
        <p class={pc}>April Dunford's core idea in <em>Obviously Awesome</em>: positioning isn't a tagline, it's the context you deliberately choose so a customer instantly understands why the product is great for them. Get it wrong and marketing, sales, and pricing all get harder downstream. The move that matters most: name the true competitive alternative (often a spreadsheet or "doing nothing," not a named competitor), then choose your frame of reference on purpose.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Pricing and packaging</h3>
        <p class={pc}>The highest-leverage decision is the <strong>value metric</strong>: what you actually charge for (per seat, per usage unit, per transaction, per outcome). Get the metric right and you can survive getting almost everything else wrong. Sequence it: define the value metric and quantify segments first, then pick an order of magnitude ($10 vs $100 vs $1K), then refine tiers, then optimise. Keep discounts under ~20%, frame annual upsells in dollars not percentages, and treat pricing as a quarterly experiment, never "done."</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Channel selection</h3>
        <div class={mcard}>
          <div class={tag}>Framework · Weinberg's Bullseye (Traction)</div>
          <h4 class="mt-2 font-sans font-bold text-navy">Find the channel that works, instead of guessing</h4>
          <ol class={olc}>
            <li><strong>What's possible:</strong> brainstorm all 19 traction channels, to counter your bias toward the two or three you already know.</li>
            <li><strong>What's probable:</strong> run cheap, fast tests on the two or three most promising, under ~$1,000 and one month each.</li>
            <li><strong>What's working:</strong> once one clearly outperforms, go all-in and optimise it, instead of spreading across several "kind of working" ones.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <p class={pc}>Most startups don't die from a bad product. They die from never systematically finding a working distribution channel. Traction deserves the same rigour as the product itself.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Crossing the chasm</h3>
        <p class={pc}>Geoffrey Moore's adoption curve runs innovators → early adopters → early majority → late majority → laggards. The <strong>chasm</strong> sits between early adopters (who buy on vision) and the early majority (who buy on proof), which is why products with real early enthusiasm often stall. Two ways across: the <strong>beachhead</strong> (dominate one narrow, winnable niche completely before expanding) and the <strong>whole product</strong> (pragmatists need the integrations, security, training, and support wrapped around the core, not just the core).</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Choosing the motion</h3>
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
            <tr><th class={th}>Motion</th><th class={th}>Fits when</th></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Product-led (PLG)</strong></td><td class={td}>Truly self-serve, buyer = user, deal size under ~$10K</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Sales-led (SLG)</strong></td><td class={td}>Complex product, high ACV, multi-stakeholder buying</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Marketing-led</strong></td><td class={td}>Usually a top-of-funnel layer feeding SLG or PLG</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Community-led</strong></td><td class={td}>An existing vocal, technical audience to amplify</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class={pc}>The pure categories are increasingly theoretical. The common real pattern is a <strong>hybrid</strong>: a simple self-serve product acquires users cheaply, then a sales layer monetises and expands upmarket. Watch four failure modes: the free adopters aren't the budget holders, the free tier gives away too much to leave anything to sell, an aggressive sales layer erodes trust with the self-serve base, and running both motions at once overwhelms a small team.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 2 -->
    <section class="border-t border-line bg-paper">
      <div class={wrap}>
        <p class={kicker}>02 · The tactics</p>
        <h2 class={h2c}>The first-customers playbook</h2>
        <p class={lede}>Zero to a real customer base, in three phases, and what actually changes at each one.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>First 10: founder-led, manual, unscalable</h3>
        <p class={pc}>Paul Graham's <em>Do Things That Don't Scale</em> is the canonical statement of this phase: startups don't take off on their own; founders have to push them over the initial hump by hand.</p>
        <div class="my-4 border-l-2 border-line py-1 pl-4">
          <div class={tag}>Stripe</div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-[0.97rem] text-ink-soft">The founders integrated Stripe live on the spot on early users' laptops (the "Collison installation"), watching confusion happen in real time and fixing it, and cut payment integration to seven lines of code.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="my-4 border-l-2 border-line py-1 pl-4">
          <div class={tag}>Airbnb</div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-[0.97rem] text-ink-soft">Cross-posted listings to Craigslist where no integration existed, emailed existing vacation-rental posters directly, and personally re-shot host photos when NYC listings underperformed, and bookings rose 2–3x, becoming an official photography program.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="my-4 border-l-2 border-line py-1 pl-4">
          <div class={tag}>Superhuman</div>
          <p class="mt-1 text-[0.97rem] text-ink-soft">Built a product-market-fit engine around one survey question ("how would you feel if you could no longer use this?") and the 40% rule (40%+ "very disappointed" = durable PMF). They moved from 22% to 58% by studying the fans and removing blockers for fence-sitters.</p>
        </div>

        <h3 class={h3c}>First 100: systematising founder-led sales</h3>
        <p class={pc}>Ad-hoc hustle becomes a lightweight but real process: build good sales habits <em>before</em> a sales team exists. Put in place a validated ICP, a basic funnel with real stages, a repeatable discovery-call structure (open with purpose, benefit, check; excavate the impact of the pain; use the prospect's own words back), and disciplined pilots offered only when a specific objection needs one.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>First 1,000+: past founder-led sales</h3>
        <p class={pc}>Close the first 10–20 customers personally before handing off, so the process is genuinely understood. The inflection is typically ~$1M ARR, or when lead volume exceeds one founder. Hire <strong>two reps at once</strong>, not one, so you can tell what's repeatable from what's one rep's quirk, and stay on as player-coach rather than exiting sales entirely.</p>

        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[480px] border-collapse text-sm">
            <tr><th class={th}>Common mistake</th><th class={th}>The fix</th></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}>"Build it and they will come"</td><td class={td}>Run the channel brainstorm in parallel with building</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}>No ICP / "spray and pray"</td><td class={td}>Interview best-fit users, mine closed-won, find situational triggers</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}>Underpricing</td><td class={td}>Nail the value metric; price to signal legitimacy, not friction</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}>Positioning by feature, not value</td><td class={td}>Name the true alternative; choose a frame of reference on purpose</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}>Founder exits sales too early</td><td class={td}>Close the first 10–20 personally; hire two reps; stay player-coach</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 3 -->
    <section class="border-t border-line">
      <div class={wrap}>
        <p class={kicker}>03 · The diagnosis</p>
        <h2 class={h2c}>Reading the founder and the startup</h2>
        <p class={lede}>The startup's shape decides which motion fits; the founder's background predicts the blind spot.</p>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Startup shapes</h3>
        <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[540px] border-collapse text-sm">
            <tr><th class={th}>Shape</th><th class={th}>Buyer</th><th class={th}>Typical early motion</th></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Horizontal SaaS</strong></td><td class={td}>A functional buyer at almost any company</td><td class={td}>Self-serve/PLG or scaled inside sales</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Vertical SaaS</strong></td><td class={td}>An owner/operator in one industry's workflow</td><td class={td}>Founder-led, high-touch, industry credibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">B2B</strong></td><td class={td}>A buying committee</td><td class={td}>Demo-and-close, content, outbound/ABM</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Marketplace</strong></td><td class={td}>Two sides with different motivations</td><td class={td}>Seed the harder side (usually supply) first</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Dev tools / API</strong></td><td class={td}>The developer, via a free tier</td><td class={td}>Docs, SDKs, community, bottoms-up</td></tr>
            <tr><td class={td}><strong class="text-navy">Hardware / deep tech</strong></td><td class={td}>Industrial/institutional buyers</td><td class={td}>Founder-led discovery for years; partnerships</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class={h3c}>Founder types</h3>
        <p class={pc}><strong>Technical vs commercial.</strong> Technical founders tend to over-build the product and under-invest in distribution. The fix is to treat selling as problem-solving, the same discipline as debugging. Commercial founders carry the inverse risk: comfortable selling, but prone to over-promising or building something closer to consulting than a product.</p>
        <p class={pc}><strong>First-time vs serial.</strong> First-timers often need explicit permission that founder-led sales is <em>their</em> job. Serial founders usually know that, but may lean on a prior playbook that doesn't match this company's buyer.</p>
        <p class={pc}><strong>The reluctant seller.</strong> A founder who built something they're proud of and finds selling it beneath the "real work": the tell-tale result is under-pricing, softened pitches, avoiding the direct ask, and delegating sales before there's a repeatable motion to hand off. There's no clean exit from sales for a founder, only a shift from doing all of it to stewarding it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- PART 4 -->
    <section class="border-t border-line bg-paper">
      <div class={wrap}>
        <p class={kicker}>04 · The field guide</p>
        <h2 class={h2c}>It changes by industry</h2>
        <p class={lede}>Four structural patterns are more transferable than memorising a separate playbook per vertical.</p>
        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-ink-soft">
          <li><strong class="text-navy">The compliance-intensity spectrum:</strong> existential in fintech and healthtech → a trust signal the buyer audits you on in cybersecurity and legal tech → a product-shaping factor in HR tech, edtech, logistics → a late-stage checklist item in dev tools, martech, e-commerce, proptech.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy">The PLG-vs-committee spectrum:</strong> dev tools sit at the pure-PLG end; healthtech, legal tech, and edtech at the pure-committee end, where a stalled "no decision" is the real risk.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy">Distribution-as-moat is vertical-specific:</strong> channel/MLS relationships in proptech, app-store marketplaces in e-commerce, carrier-network breadth in logistics, open-source community in dev tools. Knowing which lever exists in a founder's vertical is half the advice.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-navy">Fiscal and seasonal calendars are underrated:</strong> the school fiscal year in edtech, holiday code freezes in e-commerce, the spring/summer season in proptech, Q3/Q4 budgets in HR tech all impose hard timing constraints.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="border-t border-line">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 lg:py-20">
        <div class="band-dark relative overflow-hidden rounded-2xl px-8 py-14 text-center ring-1 ring-white/10 sm:px-14" data-reveal>
          <p class="font-sans text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-white/60">Apply it to your startup</p>
          <h2 class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-xl font-serif text-3xl font-medium leading-tight tracking-tight text-white sm:text-4xl">This is the reference. The work is applying it to you.</h2>
          <p class="mx-auto mt-4 max-w-lg text-white/75">The manual is the map. GTM consulting is walking it with your specific product, stage, and vertical, from diagnosis to your first paying customers.</p>
          <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
            <a href="https://selar.com/y1308v4j00" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-white px-7 py-3.5 font-sans text-sm font-semibold text-navy transition hover:bg-ivory-deep">Book a GTM Strategy Consultation <span>&rarr;</span></a>
            <a href="/book-discovery" class="inline-flex items-center rounded-sm border border-white/40 px-7 py-3.5 font-sans text-sm font-semibold text-white transition hover:bg-white hover:text-navy">Free discovery call</a>
          </div>
          <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-lg text-sm text-white/60">Bringing something sensitive? Send an NDA or mutual NDA to <a href="mailto:levi@thelevihenry.com" class="text-white underline">levi@thelevihenry.com</a> for us to sign before we meet.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-6 text-center text-sm italic text-ink-faint">Prefer the overview first? See <a href="/founders" class="text-brass-deep underline underline-offset-2">GTM consulting for founders &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
